WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.780
Every body that has a temperature radiates
energy.

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:08.300
How much energy does our sun radiate and how
much of it reaches us on earth?

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:12.420
How much energy does the Earth actually radiate
into space?

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:15.809
And what is the surface temperature of our
sun?

00:00:15.809 --> 00:00:21.090
These and similar questions can be answered
by the Stefan-Boltzmann law.

00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:26.390
This law describes the relationship between
the temperature T of a body and its radiant

00:00:26.390 --> 00:00:28.490
power P.

00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:33.890
The surface area A of the body also influences
the radiant power.

00:00:33.890 --> 00:00:41.430
The Stefan-Boltzmann law is "P is equal to
sigma times A times T to the power of four"

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:48.320
Here, "sigma" is the Stefan-Boltzmann constant
with the value "five point six seven times

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:55.489
ten to the power of minus 8, joules per meter
squared times Kelvin to the power of four,

00:00:55.489 --> 00:00:56.879
times second".

00:00:56.879 --> 00:01:03.629
This is a constant that has an exact value
that only depends on other physical constants,

00:01:03.629 --> 00:01:10.119
namely the Boltzmann constant, the Planck
constant and the speed of light.

00:01:10.119 --> 00:01:13.880
What exactly is the radiant power P?

00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:19.330
It indicates how much energy per second the
surface of the body radiates.

00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:23.800
The unit of radiant power is watt, or joule
per second.

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:29.979
In addition, the Stefan-Boltzmann law only
applies to black bodies or bodies that come

00:01:29.979 --> 00:01:31.820
close to a black body.

00:01:31.820 --> 00:01:35.750
However, the term black has nothing to do
with the color of the body.

00:01:35.750 --> 00:01:40.930
The sun, for example, is a nearly black body,
although, as you know, it is not black.

00:01:40.930 --> 00:01:44.600
A black body absorbs all the radiation that
hits it.

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:50.159
It does not reflect any radiation and does
not allow any radiation to pass through.

00:01:50.159 --> 00:01:55.930
The Stefan-Boltzmann law shows how much the
radiated energy depends on the temperature.

00:01:55.930 --> 00:02:01.440
Doubling the surface temperature of the sun
would result in 16 times more solar energy

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:03.390
reaching the earth.

00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:09.190
Let's take an example of how to determine
the temperature of the sun using the Stefan-Boltzmann

00:02:09.190 --> 00:02:10.780
law.

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:16.230
We can deduce the total amount of energy emitted
by the sun from the energy we receive from

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:18.000
the sun.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:22.980
The sun emits a power of "three point eight
times ten to the power of twenty-six joules

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:24.580
per second.

00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:30.590
The radius of the sun is "six point nine six,
times ten to the power of eight meters".

00:02:30.590 --> 00:02:34.530
Let's assume that the sun is not flat, but
spherical.

00:02:34.530 --> 00:02:39.760
We can therefore use the formula for the surface
area of a sphere to determine the surface

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:47.890
area of the sun: "A is equal to four pi times
r squared".

00:02:47.890 --> 00:02:53.250
Let's rearrange the Stefan-Boltzmann law with
respect to the temperature T and insert the

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:55.150
surface area formula.

00:02:55.150 --> 00:03:02.290
If we now insert the radiant power, the Stefan-Boltzmann
constant and the solar radius, we get "five

00:03:02.290 --> 00:03:08.640
thousand seven hundred sixty Kelvin" as the
surface temperature of the sun.

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:12.560
This corresponds to "six thousand thirty-three
degrees Celsius".

